-In this work we propose an arc-flow formulation of the problem of scheduling parallel jobs across a heterogeneous infrastructure that when solved using Gurobi is capable of solving instances with 3340 jobs to schedule across 1200 on-premises nodes and two cloud providers with 5 VM types each in under 5 minutes. The formulation takes into account data movement between on-premises and the cloud while factoring egress fees into the monetary cost. With two objective functions, one being turnaround time and the other being monetary cost the problem constitute a multi-objective optimization problem that we solve via a weighted-sum of the scalarized objectives. We also propose a genetic algorithm with local search that is able to find solutions with a mean gap of 5.66\% in under 6 seconds on average.
+In this work we deal with  a federated HPC environment, that being a computational environment composed of capacity-constrained geographically distributed on-premises resources and elastically provisioned resources from one or more cloud providers. The problem then lies in scheduling jobs between heterogeneous capacity both in terms of performance and monetary cost.
 
+Existing research addresses complementary aspects of scheduling across heterogeneous computing infrastructures, but differs in workload assumptions, resource models, and optimization objectives. Brotcorne et al.~\cite{Brotcorne2023} formulate a bi-objective optimization problem that minimizes monetary cost and makespan for bags of identical tasks in capacity-constrained multi-cloud environments. From the mathematical scheduling literature, Kramer et al.~\cite{Kramer2020} investigate weighted completion-time minimization with submission times on identical parallel machines using arc-flow formulations and decomposition methods, whereas Chen et al.~\cite{Chen2024} address unrelated parallel machines with weighted completion-time and machine-usage costs, assuming jobs are initially available. Although these studies address important components of the problem, none considers their combination within a single scheduling model. In contrast, MOSPACE jointly optimizes resource placement and scheduling for independently submitted, priority-weighted parallel jobs across finite-capacity on-premises and pay as you go cloud resources, accounting for heterogeneous execution characteristics, provisioning delays, data-transfer overheads, and associated monetary charges. This integrated formulation enables the explicit optimization of the trade-off between job turnaround and external-resource expenditure.
 
+We propose MOSPACE, a multi-objective scheduler for this Federated HPC use case. We consider independent, non-preemptive parallel jobs, each assigned to one compatible execution instance, with job-specific submission times, priorities, resource requirements, and input/output volumes. Candidate instance types have bounded availability and may differ in execution duration, provisioning time, and cost. The model assumes centralized visibility and coordination at the federation level; interoperability among autonomous local schedulers and simultaneous execution of one job across multiple sites are outside its scope.
+The main contributions are as follows:
+\begin{itemize}
+    \item A strong arc-flow formulation where homogeneous capacity are modeled as a single graph that takes into account turnaround time, costs and data movement.
+    \item A genetic algorithm that takes advantage of the nature of the problems for strong results both in terms of quality and speed.
+    \item An online simulation of the proposed solution using real data from a production cluster.
+\end{itemize}
 
