+Slurm is a widely adopted workload manager for HPC systems~\cite{Yoo2003} and provides two distinct mechanisms for operating across multiple clusters. In multi-cluster mode, jobs can be submitted to a specified set of clusters, with Slurm selecting the cluster expected to provide the earliest start time based on current scheduling estimates. In federated mode\cite{Jette2023}, jobs are replicated across eligible member clusters, whose local schedulers independently attempt to allocate resources while coordinating to ensure that each job executes only once. Although these mechanisms enable resource sharing across independently managed clusters, they do not jointly optimize global scheduling decisions against a common monetary cost and turnaround objective. MOSPACE instead formulates federation-level resource selection and scheduling of jobs as a centralized multi-objective optimization problem, explicitly accounting for heterogeneous processing characteristics, finite resource capacity, and external execution costs.
 
+A recent survey by Wu et al.~\cite{Wu2025} organizes geo-distributed scheduling research across cloud, edge, and supercomputing settings. Each environment is organized by objective (performance, fairness, fault tolerance) and by technique (heuristic, AI-based, mathematical, hybrid). Performance is divided into compute utilization and data management, the latter covering network routing, data placement and replication. The authors include HPC because its focus on extreme performance and large-scale utilization sets it apart from the other paradigms, and they position the survey as the first to span all four. Our work proposes both a heuristic and a mathematical solution where our focus is on optimizing system performance metrics ( Turnaround Time and Monetary Cost).
 
+Wang et al.~\cite{Wang2025Efficient} address job placement across geographically distributed, heterogeneous clusters with a two-level architecture in which a global coordination layer sits above autonomous clusters and makes placement decisions from a global resource view. That view combines static data (hardware specifications, capacities, topology) with dynamic data (utilization, queue lengths, network state). To make heterogeneous hardware comparable, the authors propose a standardized resource capability model. It maps CPU, memory and GPU attributes into dimensionless performance factors, each a weighted linear combination of normalized quantities: clock frequency, effective cache size, ISA and microarchitecture coefficients for CPUs; bandwidth, channel count and memory type for memory; architecture generation and peak FLOPS for GPUs.
+Placement then works as a filter-and-score heuristic. Clusters that fail hard affinity constraints (GPU type, CPU architecture, software frameworks) or lack enough normalized capacity are discarded. Each remaining cluster gets a composite score that rewards remaining compute capacity and attribute similarity and penalizes an estimated latency (startup overhead plus the time to transfer input data at the available bandwidth). Task replicas are split among clusters in proportion to the normalized scores, using a largest-remainder rounding scheme. The strategy was implemented as a Karmada scheduling plugin and evaluated on a testbed of heterogeneous clusters spread over three regions, as well as in CloudSim Plus simulations with up to twenty clusters and 800{,}000 tasks. Against Round-Robin and Karmada's default resource-aware strategy, it achieved higher and better-balanced utilization, slower growth of scheduling latency, and lower scheduling and execution failure rates.
 
+Although they share our concern with heterogeneity across federated resource pools, their approach differs from ours in several ways. Firstly they propose a online placement rule without any quality guarantee or optimization method like ours. They also fail to address  monetary cost.  Finally, the evaluation is limited to simple heuristic baselines and to operational metrics such as utilization, latency and failure rates. It does not report solution quality relative to an optimum or a bound. 
 
