 \section{Mathematical model}
 \label{sec:model}
 
-We formulate the joint selection of an instance type and a start time for each job as a bi-objective arc-flow MILP. Each instance type has a time-expanded network in which processing arcs occupy an instance for a job's full duration and waiting arcs allow idle capacity. The model minimizes priority-weighted turnaround and monetary expenditure, subject to release times and bounded instance availability.
+The scheduling of jobs in a federated environment presents a hard problem in terms of scheduling. The problem we are trying to solve is one where there are N numbers of on-premises clusters with access to M different clouds, with O jobs with different execution times and resources requirements and we aim to schedule the O jobs across the different resources so that we minimize both the cost and the total turnaround time of jobs.  We formulate the joint selection of a computing node and a start time for each job as a arc-flow MILP. Each instance type, which is any number of homogeneous non geographically distributed infrastructure, has it's own Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) in which processing arcs occupy an instance for a job's full duration and waiting arcs allow idle capacity. The model minimizes priority-weighted turnaround and monetary expenditure, subject to release times and bounded instance availability.
 
